# Why show a man from behind? Friedrich's Wanderer above the Sea of Fog
voices: {"a":"gemini:Charon | a warm, unhurried gallery guide, quietly amused"}
prompt: "What changes when we see a figure only from the back? Where does the painting put us?"
next: {"title":"Why does the street walk toward us? Caillebotte's Paris Street; Rainy Day","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/the-painting-knows-youre-there/09-why-does-the-street-walk-toward-us.cast"}

## Wanderer above the Sea of Fog: why from behind?
sources:
    - id: wiki
      title: Wanderer above the Sea of Fog
      authors: Wikipedia
      year: 2026
      finding: c. 1817/18, Hamburger Kunsthalle since 1970; dark green overcoat and walking stick; identity unknown (red hair suggests a self-portrait; others read the dress as a uniform); scene assembled from the Elbe Sandstone Mountains incl. the Zirkelstein; the most famous Rückenfigur, a device Friedrich used more than other artists; widely reproduced on book covers and posters
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wanderer_above_the_Sea_of_Fog
    - id: hk
      title: Wanderer above the Sea of Fog (HK-5161)
      authors: Hamburger Kunsthalle
      year: 2024
      finding: The painting's home since 1970
    - id: collector
      title: What Inspired Caspar David Friedrich's Wanderer Above the Sea of Fog?
      authors: TheCollector
      year: 2023
      finding: The figure as a Rückenfigur the viewer steps into; theories of his identity (a self-portrait, an officer in uniform)
      url: https://www.thecollector.com/caspar-david-friedrich-wanderer-above-sea-fog/

    card title "Wanderer above the Sea of Fog"

Caspar David Friedrich painted this wanderer around 1817,
    image art cites ["hk","wiki"] credit "Caspar David Friedrich, c. 1817. Hamburger Kunsthalle. Public domain." x 294 y 334 height 640 url https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Ueber-die-sammlung-19-jahrhundert-caspar-david-friedrich-wanderer-ueber-dem-nebelmeer.jpg/1920px-Ueber-die-sammlung-19-jahrhundert-caspar-david-friedrich-wanderer-ueber-dem-nebelmeer.jpg look screen regions.back [0.41,0.37,0.18,0.24] regions.far [0.17,0.27,0.65,0.13] regions.fog [0,0.42,1,0.4] regions.foreground [0,0.68,1,0.32] regions.head [0.455,0.335,0.075,0.06] regions.left_crags [0.07,0.455,0.29,0.2] regions.left_mountain [0.17,0.27,0.3,0.13] regions.left_ridge [0,0.37,0.44,0.08] regions.ridges [0,0.355,1,0.1] regions.right_crags [0.6,0.48,0.3,0.13] regions.right_ridge [0.6,0.36,0.4,0.1] regions.rl_a [0.03,0.36,0.02,0.02] regions.rl_b [0.385,0.405,0.02,0.02] regions.rr_a [0.95,0.355,0.02,0.02] regions.rr_b [0.6,0.43,0.02,0.02] regions.sky [0,0,1,0.27] regions.view [0,0.25,1,0.45] regions.wanderer [0.4,0.33,0.2,0.42] regions.zirkel_view [0.695,0.295,0.14,0.12] regions.zirkelstein [0.725,0.325,0.08,0.07]

and our question is: why show him only from behind?
    highlight art:wanderer strength soft

Friedrich was a German painter, living in Dresden, who painted mostly lonely landscapes.
    portrait friedrich x 760 y 360 width 220 of "Caspar David Friedrich" cameo true

    erase friedrich

A man in a dark green coat stands on a rocky peak, leaning on a walking stick, high above a sea of fog.
    highlight art:wanderer

Crags break through the fog on one side,
    highlight art:left_crags strength strong lift true

and wooded ridges on the other, like islands.
    highlight art:right_crags strength strong lift true

    camera on ["art@[0.05,0.24,0.9,0.26]"]

The view is put together from real places in the Elbe Sandstone Mountains near Dresden, sketched outdoors and rearranged in the studio.
    highlight art:left_mountain art:zirkelstein strength soft

    camera reset true

That flat-topped pillar of rock far off on the right is the Zirkelstein.
    highlight art:zirkel_view effect detail

And the man himself? Most portraits give us a face. This one g (@highlight art:head effect detail@)ives us the back of a head.

    quiz "Who is the man on the rock?" on_canvas true buttons_at.x 780 buttons_at.y 360 buttons_layout column
        right: We aren't told: the painter never says, and the man never turns round.
        wrong: Look at what the painter lets you see of him.
        * The painter
        * A soldier
        + We aren't told

Nobody knows who he is. Some point to his red hair, like Friedrich's own; others see a soldier's green uniform.
    highlight art:head art:back effect detail

A figure seen from behind even has a name in German: a [de:Rückenfigur], a back figure, and Friedrich came back to it again and again.
    highlight art:wanderer

    camera on ["art:back"]

Without a face, there is nothing to read on him: no smile, no frown, no thought.
    highlight art:head strength soft

    camera reset true

So we read his mood from what he looks at inste (@highlight art:fog strength strong lift true@)ad: the fog,

the far peaks,
    highlight art:far strength strong lift true

the open sky.
    highlight art:sky strength strong lift true

In earlier rooms, a dog, a painter and a whole royal court looked back at us. This man won't turn round.
    highlight art:wanderer lift true

    highlight art:ridges strength soft lift true duration 1.5

    ask "Where do they lead? Tap the place." spot wanderer on art
        intro: Follow the slopes of the ridges on both sides.
        right: Yes: downhill, both of them, to him.
        wrong: Trace each ridge downhill, toward the middle of the picture.

There he is: the ridges on both sides slope in toward him,
    arrow ar_l #f2c14e from.canvas [128,419] to.canvas [284,392] style.stroke_width 5
    arrow ar_r #f2c14e from.canvas [524,421] to.canvas [368,382] style.stroke_width 5
    parallel true

    erase ar_l ar_r

and he stands dead centre, just where the eye wants to go.
    highlight art:wanderer strength spotlight rest grey lift true

So where does the painting put us? Close behind him, up in the air, as if we had climbed up after him.
    highlight art:foreground strength soft lift true

We see what he sees, so we slip into his place and borrow his feelings.
    highlight art:view

    camera on ["art@[0.2,0.24,0.6,0.42]"]

And yet his back blocks the middle of the view: we are one step behind, always second.
    highlight art:back

    camera on ["art:wanderer"]

That is the double trick of the back figure: it invites you in, and it stands in your way.
    highlight art:wanderer rest paper lift true

    camera reset true

No face, no name: he could be anyone who has stood somewhere high and wondered, which is why he is on so many book covers and posters today.
    highlight art:wanderer art:view lift true

    quiz "A travel ad wants viewers to imagine themselves on a mountain top. Which shot follows Friedrich?"
        right: The back: a body to step into, and no face to make him someone else.
        wrong: Which one gives you a body to step into, without a face that makes it someone else?
        * A hiker grinning at the camera
        * The empty view, nobody in it
        + Hiker's back to us, looking out
        * A hiker's face in close-up

Film-makers and photographers still use the same shot, a figure from behind facing a view, to make us feel what they feel.
    highlight art:wanderer lift true

Next time a painting turns its back on you, ask whose place you have just been offered.
    highlight art:view strength soft

